+### JSON Patch (RFC 6902)
+
+`applyPatch(document, patch)` applies a patch, an array of operations, and returns the patched
+document:
+
+```ts
+import { applyPatch } from 'json-toolkit';
+
+const document = { items: ['first'], draft: true };
+
+applyPatch(document, [
+  { op: 'test', path: '/draft', value: true },
+  { op: 'add', path: '/items/-', value: 'second' },
+  { op: 'remove', path: '/draft' },
+]); // { items: ['first', 'second'] }
+```
+
+The operations are applied in order, each one to the document the ones before it produced. The call
+leaves `document` as it was: the result is a new value that shares no object or array with the
+document or with the patch, so later changes to the one are invisible to the other.
+
+It throws a `JsonPatchError` when the patch is not a well-formed patch document (`error.code` is
+`'invalid-patch'`), when an operation refers to a location the document does not have
+(`'unresolved'`), or when a `test` operation finds another value (`'test-failed'`). `error.index` is
+the position in the patch of the operation at fault. A patch either applies as a whole or throws:
+nothing is ever left half-patched. Notable points of the specification:
+
+- The six operations are `add`, `remove`, `replace`, `move`, `copy` and `test`; `path` holds the JSON
+  Pointer of the location the operation works on, `move` and `copy` also take a `from` pointer, and
+  `add`, `replace` and `test` a `value`. Members that the operation does not define are ignored, so
+  `{ op: 'add', path: '/a', value: 1, comment: 'why' }` is a valid operation.
+- `add` inserts before an array index, where the token `-` means after the last element, and against
+  an object it adds the member or replaces the value it already has. The container of the target has
+  to exist: adding at `/a/b` fails when there is no `/a`.
+- With the empty pointer as its `path`, `add` and `replace` make `value` the whole document. A
+  document cannot be removed, so `remove` needs a non-empty pointer.
+- `remove` and `replace` need the target to exist, and `-` names no existing element. Removing an
+  array element shifts the elements above it down.
+- `move` is a remove followed by an add, so its `path` is read against the document the removal
+  leaves behind: moving `/0` to `/-` in `['a', 'b']` yields `['b', 'a']`. A value cannot be moved
+  into one of its own children.
+- `copy` adds a copy that is independent of the original. `test` compares values structurally: the
+  order of an object's members does not matter, while `10` and `"10"` are different values.
+- As in JSON Pointer, only a document's own members are reachable: `/__proto__` is the member of that
+  name, never the prototype of anything.
+
+The `Operation` type is the union of the six operation types, discriminated by `op`, and `Patch` is a
+`readonly Operation[]`. Values whose shape is only known at runtime, such as a parsed request body,
+need a cast at the call site.
+
